{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"find /workspace -name AGENTS.md -print; wc -l paper.tex references.bib references_provenance.json claims_grounding.json paper_strategy.json global_memory_summary.md; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,205p'; cat references.bib; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"/workspace/investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111426_1794914066867660350/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111426_1794914066867660350/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111426_1794914066867660350/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111426_1794914066867660350/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111426_1794914066867660350/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111426_1794914066867660350/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n/workspace/task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111426_1794914066867660350/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n   348 paper.tex\n   199 references.bib\n   524 references_provenance.json\n   632 claims_grounding.json\n   197 paper_strategy.json\n    45 global_memory_summary.md\n  1945 total\n     1\t\\documentclass{article}\n     2\t\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n     3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption}\n     4\t\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n     5\t\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}}\n     6\t\\newcommand{\\MAE}{\\operatorname{MAE}}\n     7\t\\title{Local Residual Transfer for Budgeted\\\\Embedding Benchmark Prediction}\n     8\t\\author{}\n     9\t\n    10\t\\begin{document}\n    11\t\\maketitle\n    12\t\n    13\t\\begin{abstract}\n    14\tPredicting an embedding model's benchmark profile from a small evaluation budget requires both selecting informative benchmarks and transferring information from incomplete historical results. We study a low-rank predictor augmented with cross-fitted, locally weighted residual medians, evaluated on a pinned public MTEB score matrix. The predictor chooses queries by integrated variance reduction and calibrates residual transfer using historical models only. Across model and heuristic-family holdouts, three statistical seeds, and budgets of one, two, four, and eight queried task/subset columns, its aggregate mean absolute error is 0.04081, compared with 0.04525 for the strongest aggregate baseline. Error decreases from 0.04523 at one query to 0.03615 at eight. Historical-data disruptions delimit this finding: independent column shifts remove the advantage over column means. These results support the evaluated configuration on previously observed leaderboard cells, not causal attribution to residual transfer, prediction of genuinely missing results, or a universally sufficient evaluation budget.\n    15\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n    16\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    17\t\\end{abstract}\n    18\t\n    19\t\\section{Introduction}\n    20\tEmbedding evaluations measure different aspects of a representation, so a single score does not necessarily characterize performance on retrieval, semantic similarity, classification, and other uses. MTEB provides a public framework for evaluating this breadth \\citep{MTEB}. Running a broad evaluation suite for every new model, however, is expensive. Historical score tables suggest a complementary approach: evaluate a new model on a small set of task columns and infer its remaining profile from relationships learned across previously evaluated models. This paper examines the statistical prediction problem, not the execution cost of the underlying benchmark workloads.\n    21\t\n    22\tTwo questions must be separated. Which columns should an evaluator measure under a fixed budget? And how should the resulting observations be converted into predictions? Low-rank structure offers a compact global description, but models with similar global representations may still share systematic residual errors. A local adjustment could exploit these similarities, while incomplete overlap and small donor neighborhoods could also make it unreliable. A useful study must therefore compare the complete predictor against meaningful alternatives, keep evaluation targets independent of the query policy, and test what happens when historical relationships are disrupted.\n    23\t\n    24\tWe investigate a concrete package: ridge-regularized alternating least squares (ALS), a greedy integrated-variance query policy, and a historical-only residual calibration procedure. For a new model, the residual adjustment uses historical donors that share queried columns, weights them by profile similarity and overlap, and transfers a shrunk weighted median of their held-out residuals. The design is deliberately conservative: historical validation can choose an exactly zero correction. We hypothesize that this package will lower absolute prediction error relative to simple means, low-rank references, and linear transfer in the observed-score regime. We do not hypothesize that the residual term alone causes any measured gain.\n    25\t\n    26\tThe evidence comes from one frozen embedding dataset, not a collection of unrelated leaderboards. Its final matrix contains 126 model rows and 135 task/subset columns. Under both model and heuristic-family holdouts, predictions are scored on a common sealed target set that remains unchanged as more queries are revealed. The aggregate MAE advantage over the strongest aggregate baseline is 0.00444 in normalized score units, or 9.82\\% relative to that baseline. The same package loses its advantage over column means when historical column alignments are independently shifted. This boundary finding is part of the conclusion, rather than an exception to be excluded.\n    27\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/manifest.json]]\n    28\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/relative_reduction]]\n    29\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/delta_best_baseline]]\n    30\t\n    31\tOur contributions are a fully specified budgeted predictor, a fixed-target evaluation with dependence-aware paired uncertainty, and a measured failure boundary. Factorization, local weighting, and design have established antecedents; we do not claim novelty for these primitives or optimality of the complete configuration.\n    32\t\n    33\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    34\t\\centering\n    35\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/method_overview.png}\n    36\t\\caption{Information boundaries of budgeted score prediction. Historical scores support global factor fitting and cross-fitted donor calibration. A held-out model supplies only queried scores; these condition its latent representation and local donor weights. Sealed evaluation targets are never passed to the predictor. Query indices depend on fitted factors and earlier indices, not on the revealed score values. The diagram describes the evaluated computation, not evidence of a component-specific benefit.}\n    37\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    38\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L51-L315]]\n    39\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L911-L967]]\n    40\t\\end{figure}\n    41\t\n    42\t\\section{Related Work}\n    43\t\\paragraph{Reducing evaluation and predicting performance.}\n    44\tMTEB motivates studying broad embedding profiles rather than a single similarity task \\citep{MTEB}. Neighboring work reduces evaluation at a different granularity: tinyBenchmarks selects small sets of examples, while metabench compresses reasoning and knowledge assessments into sparse item sets \\citep{TinyBenchmarks,MetaBench}. Here the acquisition unit is an entire task/subset score, not an item response. Observational scaling laws and Sloth model performance through low-dimensional capabilities or skills across model families \\citep{ObservationalScaling,Sloth}. They motivate compact cross-benchmark structure, but our experiment neither fits a compute-scaling law nor tests those approaches as baselines. In particular, evidence from generative-model evaluations cannot establish the present embedding predictor's accuracy.\n    45\t\n    46\t\\paragraph{Incomplete matrices and local prediction.}\n    47\tFactor models and neighborhood methods are established tools for collaborative prediction \\citep{Koren2009,Takacs2008}. Probabilistic and Bayesian factorization formalize latent-factor modeling and uncertainty over parameters \\citep{Mnih2007,Salakhutdinov2008}; the present ALS estimator instead uses point estimates. Work on implicit feedback emphasizes observation semantics and confidence \\citep{Hu2008}, whereas the present mask denotes an available benchmark score, not an implicit preference. Convex matrix completion, alternating minimization, and maximum-margin factorization supply distinct approaches to incomplete matrices \\citep{Candes2009,Jain2013,Rennie2005}. Their recovery or optimization results are not guarantees for this selected leaderboard matrix. Local prediction has classical roots in neighbor classification and nonparametric regression \\citep{Cover1967,Stone1977}; quantile regression connects medians with absolute-error objectives \\citep{Koenker2001}. These connections motivate residual transfer without certifying its finite-sample behavior here.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\paragraph{Design and empirical inference.}\n    50\tBayesian experimental design and statistical active learning motivate selecting observations by expected information or prediction utility \\citep{Chaloner1995,Cohn1996}. Ridge regularization stabilizes the small queried-column regression \\citep{Hoerl1970}. Our covariance surrogate optimizes a linear variance criterion, not MAE itself. Missing-data theory cautions against interpreting observed-cell evaluation as recovery under arbitrary missingness \\citep{Rubin1976}. Bootstrap reasoning motivates uncertainty assessment \\citep{Efron1979}, but clustered dependence requires attention beyond treating rows or folds as independent \\citep{Cameron2008,Bengio2004}. We retain the evaluator's component-paired construction and report its exact meaning rather than substituting seed-level error bars for inferential uncertainty.\n    51\t\n    52\t\\section{Method}\n    53\t\\subsection{Problem setup and design rationale}\n    54\tLet $Y\\in[0,1]^{M\\times N}$ contain historical normalized scores and $\\Omega$ their observed-entry mask. A new model has score vector $y_\\star$, initially hidden. A query reservoir $R_\\star$ specifies which indices can be acquired. Given a budget $k$, the predictor selects $Q_k\\subseteq R_\\star$, observes $y_{\\star,Q_k}$, and returns an $N$-dimensional vector. The evaluation uses an evaluator-held target set $T_\\star$ disjoint from the entire reservoir, not just from the selected indices. Its primary row loss is\n    55\t\\begin{equation}\n    56\t L_\\star(k)=\\frac{1}{|T_\\star|}\\sum_{j\\in T_\\star}|\\widehat y_{\\star j}(Q_k)-y_{\\star j}|.\n    57\t \\label{eq:loss}\n    58\t\\end{equation}\n    59\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L851-L868]]\n    60\tThis target set is constant across methods and budgets. Therefore a lower loss cannot arise merely from querying and removing difficult target cells. The history contains no rows from the corresponding held-out partition, and new-model observations never update fitted historical state.\n    61\t\n    62\tThe design combines a global model with a conditional residual estimator. Global factors share information even when direct benchmark overlap is limited. Local donor profiles can represent deviations from the global fit, but missing anchors weaken neighborhood identification. We therefore use overlap-aware weights, donor-excluded predictions, effective-count shrinkage, and historical validation with a null correction. These are safeguards in the estimator, not evidence that all remaining bias or leakage is eliminated.\n    63\t\n    64\t\\subsection{Global fit and benchmark acquisition}\n    65\tThe historical mean $\\mu_j$ is computed from observed entries in column $j$, with a neutral midpoint fallback if none is observed. Rank-$r$ row vectors $u_i$ and column vectors $v_j$ are fitted by alternating ridge solves for\n    66\t\\begin{equation}\n    67\t \\min_{U,V}\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\Omega}(Y_{ij}-\\mu_j-u_i^\\top v_j)^2\n    68\t       +\\lambda\\left(\\sum_i\\|u_i\\|_2^2+\\sum_j\\|v_j\\|_2^2\\right).\n    69\t \\label{eq:als}\n    70\t\\end{equation}\n    71\tThe evaluated settings are $r=4$, $\\lambda=0.1$, and 50 sweeps; column factors start from independent zero-mean Gaussian entries with standard deviation 0.1. The historical means remain fixed. Only observed entries participate in the alternating solves; missing entries are not treated as observed zero scores.\n    72\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L363-L388]]\n    73\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L920-L939]]\n    74\t\n    75\tFor queried indices $Q$, define\n    76\t\\begin{equation}\n    77\t C_Q=(\\lambda I+V_Q^\\top V_Q)^{-1},\\qquad\n    78\t z_\\star=C_Q V_Q^\\top(y_{\\star,Q}-\\mu_Q),\\qquad\n    79\t b_\\star=\\clip(\\mu+Vz_\\star).\n    80\t \\label{eq:condition}\n    81\t\\end{equation}\n    82\tHere $V$ has row $v_j^\\top$. For each unqueried reservoir index $a$, acquisition maximizes\n    83\t\\begin{equation}\n    84\t g(a\\mid Q)=\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^N(v_j^\\top C_Qv_a)^2}{1+v_a^\\top C_Qv_a}.\n    85\t \\label{eq:query}\n    86\t\\end{equation}\n    87\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L395-L426]]\n    88\tThis follows by substituting the rank-one update\n    89\t$C_{Q\\cup\\{a\\}}=C_Q-C_Qv_av_a^\\top C_Q/(1+v_a^\\top C_Qv_a)$\n    90\tinto the reduction of $\\operatorname{tr}(VC_QV^\\top)$. Thus the score is an exact reduction of this surrogate, with unit observation-noise convention, for the frozen factors. It is not a calibrated predictive posterior or a guarantee of lower realized absolute error. The sum includes \\emph{all} columns, including the acquired column; it is not a target-only acquisition objective. The ordering depends on factors, the reservoir, and selected indices, so score feedback affects prediction but does not change subsequent index choices.\n    91\t\n    92\t\\subsection{Cross-fitted local residual transfer}\n    93\tHistorical donors are partitioned into folds. For each donor fold, an auxiliary factor model is fitted without those donors. A donor's available entries among $Q$ condition that model using Equation~\\ref{eq:condition}; its finite observed target entries give residuals $e_{ij}(Q)=Y_{ij}-b^{(-i)}_{ij}(Q)$. The superscript denotes fold exclusion, not a separate fit for every donor. An unobserved donor target contributes no residual. Auxiliary predictions and the main base prediction are clipped before residual calculation or adjustment.\n    94\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L97-L142]]\n    95\t\n    96\tFor donor $i$, let $A_i=Q\\cap\\Omega_i$ be its shared queried anchors and $s_{ia}$ the standard deviation of column $a$ in that donor's auxiliary fit, floored at 0.025. At bandwidth $h$, the profile distance and weight are\n    97\t\\begin{equation}\n    98\t d_i^2=\\frac{\\sum_{a\\in A_i}[(Y_{ia}-y_{\\star a})/s_{ia}]^2}{\\max(|A_i|,1)},\\qquad\n    99\t w_i=\\frac{|A_i|}{|Q|}\\exp[-d_i^2/(2h^2)].\n   100\t \\label{eq:weights}\n   101\t\\end{equation}\n   102\tWeights are zero without shared anchors. For column $j$, retain only donors with finite $e_{ij}$ and positive $w_i$, denoting this set $D_j$. Compute\n   103\t\\begin{equation}\n   104\t m_j=\\operatorname{wmed}_{i\\in D_j}(e_{ij};w_i),\\quad\n   105\t n_j^{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\frac{(\\sum_{i\\in D_j}w_i)^2}{\\sum_{i\\in D_j}w_i^2},\\quad\n   106\t \\widehat y_{\\star j}=\\clip\\left(b_{\\star j}+\\alpha\\frac{n_j^{\\mathrm{eff}}}{n_j^{\\mathrm{eff}}+\\tau}m_j\\right).\n   107\t \\label{eq:correction}\n   108\t\\end{equation}\n   109\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L145-L197]]\n   110\tThe weighted median is the smallest sorted residual whose cumulative weight reaches half the total. It minimizes weighted absolute residual loss, but the subsequent shrinkage and clipping mean that Equation~\\ref{eq:correction} is not generally that minimizer. A negligible total weight leaves the base unchanged. Queried entries are not residual-adjusted. Effective count measures weight concentration, not geometric support: many equally weighted distant donors can still have a large count. No extrapolation guarantee follows from this statistic.\n   111\t\n   112\t\\subsection{Historical calibration and computational scope}\n   113\tThe correction is tuned without access to the held-out new model. Historical validation rows are split into two folds. Each validation fold is excluded from both its auxiliary base fit and its nested donor bank; donors are also excluded from the fits used to obtain their residuals. Querying is simulated within each historical validation row. Absolute errors are pooled over its finite unqueried cells at the same reporting budgets, then minimized over bandwidths $\\{0.35,0.75,1.5\\}$, shrinkages $\\{4,16\\}$, and blends $\\{0,0.25,0.5,1\\}$. Ties within the implemented tolerance favor the zero blend when it attains the minimum. The final donor bank is rebuilt from history after calibration.\n   114\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L200-L297]]\n   115\tThis tuning target differs from the outer fixed-target loss: historical validation scores all available unqueried cells and pools cells, whereas the outer score averages row losses on sealed targets. The historical objective is a selection heuristic, not an unbiased estimate of the outer objective. There is no released outer comparison against the auxiliary unconditional-residual or direct-donor diagnostics computed during tuning.\n   116\t\n   117\tCalibration caps the pool at $\\min(512,\\max(64,\\lfloor196608/N\\rfloor))$ rows, uses at most 128 donors and 16 validation rows, and limits auxiliary ALS to 12 sweeps. These are source-defined work limits, not measured runtime claims. For $E$ observed cells, one ALS sweep has dense-solve cost $O(Er^2+(M+N)r^3)$; auxiliary fits multiply this cost. Donor residual storage is bounded by donor count times column count, and query conditioning solves a small ridge system. These algebraic costs explain CPU feasibility at this matrix scale without establishing the storage or runtime of a full upstream leaderboard acquisition.\n   118\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L200-L229]]\n   119\t\n   120\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n   121\t\\subsection{Data, normalization, and information access}\n   122\tThe public results snapshot is fixed to MTEB revision\n   123\t\\texttt{6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383}.\n   124\tThe retained final matrix has 126 rows and 135 columns, where a column identifies task, dataset revision, split, subset, and metric. These are not 135 independent benchmark suites. Extraction retains supported English text-only main metrics, checks their agreement with the reported main score, and chooses a common supported revision/metric/split per task/subset under the recorded coverage rule. Per-model revision selection favors coverage. Bounded noncorrelation scores remain in $[0,1]$; correlations are mapped by $(x+1)/2$. Hence errors are in normalized score units, not percentage-point errors uniformly comparable across raw metrics.\n   125\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/manifest.json]]\n   126\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L685-L713]]\n   127\t\n   128\tCanonicalized result content, row duplicates, aliases, and heuristic lexical-lineage families are used in partition construction. Model holdout excludes row, alias, and normalized-document overlap between history and test; family holdout additionally excludes the recorded family labels. The latter are heuristics, not verified model ancestry. Development and final partitions are also checked for the recorded identity, family, and content separation. Such checks reduce specified overlaps but cannot establish independence of training corpora or undocumented ancestry.\n   129\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L715-L770]]\n   130\t\n   131\tFor each new-model row, the observed columns are shuffled deterministically and divided into a query reservoir containing approximately two thirds and a sealed remainder. The cut is at least eight columns, and each retained row has enough support for a nonempty target set. Targets and the complete target mask are withheld. The predictor receives an opaque indexed historical matrix, the queryable indices, and only the scores it requests. It cannot query genuinely missing cells in this experiment. Thus the study is a retrospective prediction test conditional on historical reporting, not evidence that unreported leaderboard scores can be recovered without selection bias.\n   132\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L851-L863]]\n   133\t\n   134\t\\subsection{Comparators and matched controls}\n   135\tThe five references span simple, low-rank, and direct linear prediction. \\emph{Column mean} returns the historical mean for each target. \\emph{Query mean} assigns the new model's mean acquired score to every column. Both use variance-ranked queries where relevant. \\emph{Random low-rank} and \\emph{variance low-rank} fit the same rank and ridge family and condition on randomly ordered or historical-variance-ranked queries. \\emph{Linear ridge} regresses centered target columns on the variance-ranked acquired columns, zero-filling missing centered historical entries in both its design and responses. Predictions are clipped where implemented. These low-rank references use different query policies from the studied package and therefore are not residual-only ablations.\n   136\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L833-L902]]\n   137\t\n   138\tTwo controls reuse the model-holdout partitions and sealed targets. In the \\emph{constant-history} condition every observed historical value is replaced by its column mean, preserving the mask. In the \\emph{column-shifted} condition each column's values and masks are rolled together by an independently drawn nonzero row offset. This preserves the within-column observed-value multiset but perturbs cross-column alignment. Every method is refitted to the transformed history; the held-out model's answers are unchanged. These are data interventions on the full prediction system, not isolated interventions on residual transfer, and they do not guarantee removal of every dependence.\n   139\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L808-L830]]\n   140\t\n   141\t\\subsection{Aggregation, uncertainty, and qualification}\n   142\tReporting budgets are $k\\in\\{1,2,4,8\\}$, using nested query prefixes. Statistical seeds are 6841, 8089, and 9011. Most conditions use 94 historical and 32 test rows; the last family-holdout split uses 93 and 33. Within each condition and seed, losses average targets within a row, rows within a split, and the four budgets equally. The principal aggregate averages the two holdouts and three seeds equally. The strongest baseline is chosen by the minimum of these aggregate losses, not separately per row or budget. RMSE is the square root of the equally aggregated squared-error losses.\n   143\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/plan.json]]\n   144\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L970-L996]]\n   145\t\n   146\tFor uncertainty, the evaluator draws 400 bootstrap multiplicity vectors over the union of recorded dependence components, with random seed 424242. A draw reweights the same component consistently wherever it appears across seeds and holdouts. Within each split, paired baseline-minus-studied row errors, averaged over budgets, are reweighted and normalized before equal split averaging. For each of five baselines, the 0.005 and 0.995 quantiles form the evaluator's Bonferroni-style bounds. The reported pair takes the minimum of the five lower bounds and separately the minimum of the five upper bounds. This is \\emph{not} a conventional interval for a single prespecified comparator or the quantiles of a minimum-gain statistic. Finite bootstrap resolution, heuristic components, and shared data remain inferential limitations.\n   147\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L999-L1017]]\n   148\t\n   149\tThe independent frozen evaluation completed and passed its qualification checks. Reset replay had identical query indices and maximum absolute prediction difference zero. These protocol checks do not certify universal validity or absence of every leakage source. Numerical displays use retained evaluator-owned losses, not unverified predictor diagnostics; no new model evaluation is performed for this paper.\n   150\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status]]\n   151\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n   152\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/reset_max_abs_delta]]\n   153\t\n   154\t\\section{Results}\n   155\t\\subsection{Main fixed-target comparison}\n   156\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   157\t\\centering\n   158\t\\input{tables/main_comparison.tex}\n   159\t\\caption{Sealed-target MAE, lower is better, averaged equally over reporting budgets and seeds. Overall gives equal weight to model and heuristic-family holdouts. All five evaluated reference methods are included. Different acquisition policies prevent treating low-rank references as component ablations.}\n   160\t\\label{tab:main}\n   161\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   162\t\\end{table}\n   163\t\n   164\tThe studied package attains aggregate MAE 0.04081 and RMSE 0.06432. Linear ridge is the strongest aggregate reference at MAE 0.04525, giving an absolute reduction of 0.00444 and relative reduction of 9.82\\%. Table~\\ref{tab:main} shows lower aggregate loss for the studied configuration than all five references in each holdout. This is a comparison between complete evaluated configurations. It does not identify which part of their fitting, acquisition, or prediction differences accounts for the reduction.\n   165\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n   166\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/primary_mean]]\n   167\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/relative_reduction]]\n   168\t\n   169\tThe holdout distinction matters. The package's MAE is 0.03831 under model holdout and 0.04330 under heuristic-family holdout, whereas linear ridge gives 0.04512 and 0.04538. Removing recorded family overlap therefore coincides with a narrower advantage. Since these partitions also differ in their model composition, this contrast is not a controlled causal estimate of family membership or a demonstrated robustness guarantee for unseen true lineages.\n   170\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/cases]]\n   171\t\n   172\tThe package's seed-level pooled MAEs are 0.03850, 0.04113, and 0.04280 (sample SD 0.00217), versus linear ridge's 0.04166, 0.04594, and 0.04815. These shared-data replications all favor the package. Component-bootstrap bound minima are $[0.00210,0.00920]$. Their positive lower minimum supports the aggregate comparisons under the specified construction; the endpoints need not correspond to the same baseline.\n   173\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/primary_seed]]\n   174\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/primary_seed_sd]]\n   175\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/gain_ci_low]]\n   176\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/gain_ci_high]]\n   177\t\n   178\t\\subsection{Accuracy versus query budget}\n   179\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   180\t\\centering\n   181\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/budget_comparison.pdf}\n   182\t\\caption{Fixed-target MAE versus acquisition budget for model holdout (a) and heuristic-family holdout (b). Points average seeds; bars are descriptive sample standard deviations across the three seeds, not confidence intervals. Both panels share axes and retain the same targets across budgets. Simple references appear in Table~\\ref{tab:main}; complete budget values are in the supplement.}\n   183\t\\label{fig:budget}\n   184\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   185\t\\end{figure}\n   186\t\n   187\tAcross the two primary conditions, the package's MAEs at one, two, four, and eight queries are 0.04523, 0.04221, 0.03964, and 0.03615. Figure~\\ref{fig:budget} separates the holdouts rather than masking their difference in a single curve. Their seed-averaged errors decrease at every reported budget; the same monotonicity is not asserted for every individual model. At eight queries, the package gives 0.03448 on model holdout and 0.03782 on family holdout, compared with linear ridge's 0.03927 and 0.03984.\n   188\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/mae_k1]]\n   189\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/mae_k2]]\n   190\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/mae_k4]]\n   191\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/mae_k8]]\n   192\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/cases]]\n   193\t\n   194\tMore queried columns improve the reported aggregate over the tested range, but do not identify a universally adequate $k$. No application-specific tolerance or per-column execution costs were specified, and larger budgets were not measured. Moreover, the acquisition surrogate optimizes linear predictive variance, not sealed-target MAE.\n   195\t\n   196\t\\subsection{Dependence on historical structure}\n   197\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   198\t\\centering\n   199\t\\includegraphics[width=.9\\textwidth]{figures/history_structure_controls.pdf}\n   200\t\\caption{Matched model-holdout controls, averaged over budgets and seeds; bars show descriptive seed standard deviations. Constant history replaces each observed value by its historical column mean. Column-shifted history independently cyclically shifts each column's value/mask pairs. Small horizontal offsets separate overlapping points. The studied package does not beat column means after shifting; these conditions are not residual-only ablations.}\n   201\t\\label{fig:controls}\n   202\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   203\t\\end{figure}\n   204\t\n   205\tWith intact history under model holdout, the package's MAE is 0.03831. Replacing historical observed values by column means raises it to 0.06437, equal to the column-mean and variance-low-rank references. Independently shifting columns yields MAE 0.06857, compared with 0.06437 for column means and 0.07091 for variance low-rank. Thus the package retains an advantage over the latter reference but is worse than the simpler historical mean in this disrupted condition. The appropriate intact comparator here is model holdout, not the pooled principal score.\n@article{MTEB,\n author = {Niklas Muennighoff and Nouamane Tazi and Loïc Magne and Nils Reimers},\n title = {{MTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark}},\n year = {2023},\n journal = {Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics},\n doi = {10.18653/v1/2023.eacl-main.148}\n}\n\n@article{TinyBenchmarks,\n author = {Felipe Maia Polo and Lucas Weber and Leshem Choshen and Yuekai Sun and Gongjun Xu and Mikhail Yurochkin},\n title = {{tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples}},\n year = {2024},\n journal = {arXiv (Cornell University)},\n doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2402.14992}\n}\n\n@article{MetaBench,\n author = {Alex Kipnis and Konstantinos Voudouris and Luca M. Schulze Buschoff and Eric Schulz},\n title = {{metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models}},\n year = {2024},\n journal = {arXiv (Cornell University)},\n doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2407.12844}\n}\n\n@article{ObservationalScaling,\n author = {Yangjun Ruan and Chris J. Maddison and Tatsunori Hashimoto},\n title = {{Observational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Language Model Performance}},\n year = {2024},\n journal = {neural information processing systems},\n doi = {10.52202/079017-0506}\n}\n\n@article{Sloth,\n author = {Felipe Maia Polo and Seamus Somerstep and Leshem Choshen and Yuekai Sun and Mikhail Yurochkin},\n title = {{Sloth: scaling laws for LLM skills to predict multi-benchmark performance across families}},\n year = {2025},\n journal = {neural information processing systems},\n doi = {10.52202/085713-1318}\n}\n\n@article{Koren2009,\n author = {Yehuda Koren and Robert Bell and Chris T. Volinsky},\n title = {{Matrix Factorization Techniques for Recommender Systems}},\n year = {2009},\n journal = {Computer},\n doi = {10.1109/mc.2009.263}\n}\n\n@article{Hu2008,\n author = {Yifan Hu and Yehuda Koren and Chris T. Volinsky},\n title = {{Collaborative Filtering for Implicit Feedback Datasets}},\n year = {2008},\n journal = {Proceedings},\n doi = {10.1109/icdm.2008.22}\n}\n\n@article{Candes2009,\n author = {Emmanuel J. Candès and Benjamin Recht},\n title = {{Exact Matrix Completion via Convex Optimization}},\n year = {2009},\n journal = {Foundations of Computational Mathematics},\n doi = {10.1007/s10208-009-9045-5}\n}\n\n@article{Jain2013,\n author = {Prateek Jain and Praneeth Netrapalli and Sujay Sanghavi},\n title = {{Low-rank Matrix Completion using Alternating Minimization}},\n year = {2012},\n journal = {arXiv (Cornell University)},\n doi = {10.48550/arxiv.1212.0467}\n}\n\n@article{Rennie2005,\n author = {Jasson D. M. Rennie and Nathan Srebro},\n title = {{Fast maximum margin matrix factorization for collaborative prediction}},\n year = {2005},\n journal = {International Conference on Machine Learning},\n doi = {10.1145/1102351.1102441}\n}\n\n@article{Koenker2001,\n author = {Roger W. Koenker and Kevin F. Hallock},\n title = {{Quantile Regression}},\n year = {2001},\n journal = {The Journal of Economic Perspectives},\n doi = {10.1257/jep.15.4.143}\n}\n\n@article{Stone1977,\n author = {Charles J. Stone},\n title = {{Consistent Nonparametric Regression}},\n year = {1977},\n journal = {The Annals of Statistics},\n doi = {10.1214/aos/1176343886}\n}\n\n@article{Bengio2004,\n author = {Yoshua Bengio and Yves Grandvalet},\n title = {{No Unbiased Estimator of the Variance of K-Fold Cross-Validation}},\n year = {2004},\n journal = {Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n doi = {10.5555/1005332.1044695}\n}\n\n@article{Hoerl1970,\n author = {Arthur E. Hoerl and Robert W. Kennard},\n title = {{Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems}},\n year = {1970},\n journal = {Technometrics},\n doi = {10.1080/00401706.1970.10488634}\n}\n\n@article{Chaloner1995,\n author = {Kathryn M. Chaloner and Isabella Verdinelli},\n title = {{Bayesian Experimental Design: A Review}},\n year = {1995},\n journal = {Statistical Science},\n doi = {10.1214/ss/1177009939}\n}\n\n@article{Cohn1996,\n author = {David A. Cohn and Zoubin Ghahramani and Michael I. Jordan},\n title = {{Active Learning with Statistical Models}},\n year = {1996},\n journal = {Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research},\n doi = {10.1613/jair.295}\n}\n\n@article{Rubin1976,\n author = {Donald B. Rubin},\n title = {{Inference and Missing Data}},\n year = {1976},\n journal = {Biometrika},\n doi = {10.1093/biomet/63.3.581}\n}\n\n@article{Efron1979,\n author = {Bradley Efron},\n title = {{Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife}},\n year = {1979},\n journal = {The Annals of Statistics},\n doi = {10.1214/aos/1176344552}\n}\n\n@article{Cameron2008,\n author = {A. Colin Cameron and Jonah B. Gelbach and Douglas L. Miller},\n title = {{Bootstrap-Based Improvements for Inference with Clustered Errors}},\n year = {2008},\n journal = {The Review of Economics and Statistics},\n doi = {10.1162/rest.90.3.414}\n}\n\n@article{Takacs2008,\n author = {Gábor Takács and István Pilászy and Bottyán Németh and Domonkos Tikk},\n title = {{Matrix factorization and neighbor based algorithms for the netflix prize problem}},\n year = {2008},\n journal = {ACM Conference on Recommender Systems (RecSys)},\n doi = {10.1145/1454008.1454049}\n}\n\n@article{Mnih2007,\n author = {Andriy Mnih and Ruslan Salakhutdinov},\n title = {{Probabilistic Matrix Factorization}},\n year = {2007},\n journal = {UCL Discovery (University College London)},\n doi = {}\n}\n\n@article{Salakhutdinov2008,\n author = {Ruslan Salakhutdinov and Andriy Mnih},\n title = {{Bayesian probabilistic matrix factorization using Markov chain Monte Carlo}},\n year = {2008},\n journal = {International Conference on Machine Learning},\n doi = {10.1145/1390156.1390267}\n}\n\n@article{Cover1967,\n author = {Thomas M. Cover and Peter E. Hart},\n title = {{Nearest neighbor pattern classification}},\n year = {1967},\n journal = {IEEE Transactions on Information Theory},\n doi = {10.1109/tit.1967.1053964}\n}\n\n@article{Harris2020,\n author = {Charles R. Harris and K. Jarrod Millman and Stéfan J. van der Walt and Ralf Gommers and Pauli Virtanen and David Cournapeau and Eric Wieser and Julian Taylor and Sebastian Berg and Nathaniel J. Smith and Robert Kern and Matti Picus and Stephan Hoyer and M. H. van Kerkwijk and Matthew Brett and Allan Haldane and Jaime Fernández del Río and Mark Wiebe and Pearu Peterson and Pierre Gérard-Marchant and Kevin Sheppard and Tyler Reddy and Warren Weckesser and Hameer Abbasi and Christoph Gohlke and Travis E. Oliphant},\n title = {{Array programming with NumPy}},\n year = {2020},\n journal = {Nature},\n doi = {10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2}\n}\n\n@article{Hunter2007,\n author = {John D. Hunter},\n title = {{Matplotlib: A 2D Graphics Environment}},\n year = {2007},\n journal = {Computing in Science \\& Engineering},\n doi = {10.1109/mcse.2007.55}\n}\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Generated Overlap-Aware Transfer Design and matching bundles; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No comparative execution evidence was produced, so no distilled recommendation is warranted.\n  - evidence: The documented task provides sparse historical benchmark scores and k observed scores for a new model. Bootstrap manifest reports correct=true and combined_score=0.9497689627225859; the named candidate source file was unavailable. Histories and authoritative campaign JSONL were empty. No evaluator code, prohibited literature, task program or numerical experiment was accessed or executed. Low-rank D-optimal design remains a comparator; nonlinear complexity was deferred. Canonical local memory is ideas/01_overlap_aware_transfer_design/memory.md and no alternate local target was used.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T12:51:06.006439+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.9470120058952767, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"baseline_mae\": 0.05679157357761161, \"combined_score\": 0.9470120058952767, \"constant_control_mae\": 0.09437180052909647, \"correct\": true, \"delta_best_baseline\": 0.003803579472888341, \"mae\": 0.05298799410472327, \"permuted_control_mae\": 0.09420604980762494, \"reset_max_abs_delta\": 0.0, \"rmse\": 0.0851395755758941}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Created Cross-Fitted Local Residual Medians and matching bundles; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=One aggregate prior comparison does not identify a causal mechanism or validate the proposed correction, so no distilled method recommendation is warranted.\n  - evidence: Prior correct=true and run_exit_code=0 with combined_score=0.9470120058952767 versus incumbent 0.9497689627225859; prior MAE=0.05298799410472327 versus its reported baseline_mae=0.05679157357761161. Overall rubric pass was false; no component-specific causal diagnosis is supplied. The named incumbent source file was unavailable. No new candidate/evaluator/scientific execution or prohibited-literature access occurred. Deferred another covariance/query rewrite and adaptive particle selection; selected prediction-only local residual transfer with direct-neighbor and unconditional-residual controls. Canonical memory is ideas/02_cross_fitted_local_residual_medians/memory.md, the only cycle-2 local memory target created or used.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T13:05:43.427057+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.9540726679067297, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"baseline_mae\": 0.05679157357761161, \"combined_score\": 0.9540726679067297, \"constant_control_mae\": 0.09437180052909647, \"correct\": true, \"delta_best_baseline\": 0.0108642414843413, \"mae\": 0.04592733209327031, \"permuted_control_mae\": 0.09799393071953512, \"reset_max_abs_delta\": 0.0, \"rmse\": 0.0732945885907783}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Created Support-Calibrated Residual Transport and matching bundles; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No new experiment or mechanism-isolating evidence was obtained, and the single accepted aggregate comparison does not establish which component transfers reliably.\n  - evidence: Cycle 2 passed with combined_score=0.9540726679067297, MAE=0.04592733209327031, correct=true. Inspected its results-relative candidate source and method note only. Effective donor count measures relative weight concentration rather than geometric support; geometric gating remains untested. Alternatives considered: budget-specific blending, local-linear correction, and query-policy changes. Chose target-specific distance gating to isolate a prediction-side mechanism without extra fits. No new task execution or scientific measurements.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown