"normalized_title\": \"tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples\",\n      \"author_names\": [\n        \"Felipe Maia Polo\",\n        \"Lucas Weber\",\n        \"Leshem Choshen\",\n        \"Yuekai Sun\",\n        \"Gongjun Xu\",\n        \"Mikhail Yurochkin\"\n      ],\n      \"year\": 2024,\n      \"venue\": \"arXiv (Cornell University)\",\n      \"query\": \"ARXIV:2402.14992\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.14992?select=id,doi,title,publication_year,authorships,abstract_inverted_index,primary_location,biblio,type\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:02:28.801543+00:00\",\n      \"consulted_content\": \"abstract\",\n      \"evidence_path\": \"literature_evidence/TinyBenchmarks.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.openalex.W4406058560\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"https://openalex.org/W4406058560\",\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Final evaluation completed and qualified; qualification is not evidence of universal validity or superiority.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.qualification.status\",\n        \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n        \"final.qualification.correct\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The final frozen MTEB plane has 126 rows and 135 task/subset columns, not independent benchmark suites; results revision is 6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"source_locations\": [\n        \"source_evidence/resources/final/manifest.json:1\",\n        \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:715\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"English text-only main-score extraction preserves bounded noncorrelations and maps correlations by (x+1)/2; task-definition revision 5a83aafe4e7140d0606b96f59448c704f31dbf76, global support 32 and row support 12 are manifest rules.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"source_locations\": [\n        \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:685\",\n        \"source_evidence/resources/final/manifest.json:1\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"History/test alias and normalized content separation are checked; family holdout additionally separates heuristic family labels. This does not certify true ancestry or pretraining independence.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"source_locations\": [\n        \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:715\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"A shuffled approximately two-thirds reservoir, cut at least eight, is disjoint from a sealed target set fixed across budgets and methods; only observed cells are retrospectively evaluated.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"source_locations\": [\n        \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:851\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Rank-four ridge ALS with ridge 0.1, fifty sweeps and Gaussian factor initialization SD 0.1 fits observed entries only; historical means remain fixed.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.best_correct_score\",\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"source_locations\": [\n        \"source_evidence/selected/src/program.py:363\",\n        \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:920\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Ridge conditioning and the all-column integrated-variance acquisition formula describe the evaluated source. The rank-one algebra is a surrogate reduction, not an MAE or calibrated posterior guarantee; selection is independent of revealed values.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.best_correct_score\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"source_locations\": [\n        \"source_evidence/selected/src/program.py:395\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Donor-excluded auxiliary predictions yield residuals only for observed donor targets; shared-anchor kernel weights are multiplied by overlap fraction, use a 0.025 scale floor, and vanish at zero overlap.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.best_correct_score\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"source_locations\": [\n        \"source_evidence/selected/src/program.py:51\",\n        \"source_evidence/selected/src/program.py:97\",\n        \"source_evidence/selected/src/program.py:145\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Weighted median residual correction uses effective-count shrinkage, excludes queried columns, clips to [0,1], and leaves the base unchanged below total-weight threshold 1e-12 or at zero blend.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.best_correct_score\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"source_locations\": [\n        \"source_evidence/selected/src/program.py:164\",\n        \"source_evidence/selected/src/program.py:300\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"History-only nested calibration uses two validation folds and two donor folds, at most 16 validation rows, 128 donors, 12 auxiliary sweeps, and pool cap min(512,max(64,floor(196608/N))).\",\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Local Residual Transfer for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How accurately can a sparse historical embedding-score matrix and one to eight queried scores predict a held-out model's sealed benchmark scores under model and heuristic-family holdouts, and how does disrupting historical score structure change that accuracy?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the pinned MTEB observed-score evaluation, the frozen low-rank predictor with cross-fitted local residual transfer has lower aggregate MAE than the five evaluated baselines and decreasing aggregate error across the tested query budgets, while history-disruption controls expose limits to this advantage without isolating the contribution of residual transfer.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the completed independent final evaluation, not the search ordering: MAE 0.040807843083862115 versus the minimum aggregate baseline MAE 0.045249969215420516, with budgets 1, 2, 4, 8 and two primary holdout conditions across three seeds. The final evidence also contains evaluator-computed row/checkpoint losses for all five baselines and matched constant-history and column-shift controls. This supports one scoped empirical method study, not a proof of optimal benchmark selection, a residual-component superiority claim, or a generative-LLM claim. Dataset conditions are evidence blocks under one question, not separate discoveries. No downstream_study object is present; final evidence was read independently.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Fresh framing, not an edit-mode replacement: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper directory, prior manuscript, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is available. The rejected option is importing an assumed prior story from memory; there is no prior manuscript to preserve. Compare candidate framings explicitly: a search-success story is rejected as chronological and nonconfirmatory; a broad cross-setting method-family story is rejected because only one pinned embedding dataset is evaluated; a scoped prediction-and-limits story is selected because it matches the final evidence and selected implementation.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"The selected implementation is directly available at results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_local_residual_medians/program_1/src/program.py with RESIDUAL_METHOD.md; result.best_correct_score and results/best_program_manifest.json identify this selection. Mechanism description is grounded by inspection, not inferred from the variant name.\",\n    \"The final manifest and evaluator establish a 126-row, 135-task/subset-column final plane from the pinned MTEB snapshot; this narrows the original broad language-model motivation to text-embedding score prediction. These are not 135 independent benchmark suites or the entire public leaderboard.\",\n    \"Final row/checkpoint arrays permit direct budget, holdout and control comparisons; absent outer component ablations prevent claiming residual correction causes the observed gain. Search-only alternatives do not become matched final ablations.\",\n    \"final.qualification.status is completed and final.qualification.outcome is qualified. This is a protocol/evaluation outcome, not proof of novelty, generalization, or performance at every budget. No failed final qualification is present in this run; any later not_qualified outcome must remain disclosed even if metrics are retained.\",\n    \"The references files are present, but available retrieval metadata is not a substitute for reading a paper or support for detailed related-work claims; figure assets are currently absent. This stage plans figures rather than claiming they exist.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Low-rank prediction with cross-fitted local residual transfer\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The frozen studied method: observed-entry ridge ALS, greedy all-column posterior-variance reduction for querying, ridge inference for the new row, and overlap-aware kernel-weighted donor residual medians with history-only tuning and zero-blend fallback. Describe the full evaluated configuration rather than attributing its performance to one unablated component.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Low-rank prediction with random or variance-ranked queries\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Two evaluator-run comparators share the low-rank prediction family but use random or historical-column-variance query ordering. They contextualize the evaluated package, not a clean residual-only or query-only ablation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Linear ridge transfer\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The evaluator fits ridge transfer from queried scores to all columns using observed historical values with missing centered entries filled by zero; its variance-ranked queries and distinct fitting procedure must be stated. Retain this fixed comparator in main results without inventing an unevaluated matched greedy-ALS baseline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Column means and observed-score mean\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The two simple references distinguish a query-free historical prior from copying the new model's queried-score mean across targets. Include both in the main numeric comparison even when the latter is weak; do not omit unfavorable or uninteresting baseline evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Constant-history and independently column-shifted history\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"These are matched data-disruption conditions, not new methods or isolated component ablations. Constant observed values preserve masks; cyclic shifts of each column's value/mask pairs preserve column marginals while disrupting row alignment. They establish a descriptive boundary on the intact-history results, not causal identification or a hidden-adaptivity test.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Main table: all five baseline methods and the studied method, in a predefined scientific grouping, with overall fixed-target MAE and condition-specific MAE. Report the final RMSE 0.06432104648676243 only for the studied method unless comparator RMSE is actually available; never manufacture baseline RMSE.\",\n    \"Primary aggregate: final.metric.mae = 0.040807843083862115; final.metric.baseline_mae = 0.045249969215420516; final.metric.delta_best_baseline = 0.004442126131558401. The baseline minimum is chosen once after the same overall aggregation, not separately per row, budget or condition. Keep search scores out of this table.\",\n    \"Budget comparison: aggregate MAEs at k=1/2/4/8 are 0.04523334197776794, 0.0422085090615931, 0.03963906108320536, 0.03615046021288207. Plot separate model-holdout and heuristic-family-holdout panels from final.evidence.episodes with the same axes. These are observation-budget comparisons, not optimization trajectories; do not infer that eight queries are sufficient for an unspecified tolerance.\",\n    \"Uncertainty: report seed-level values descriptively and preserve paired component resampling. The supplied gain bounds 0.0021032442082077377 and 0.009199712663056218 are minima across five baseline-specific bounds from 400 component bootstrap draws, using quantiles 0.005/0.995 and RNG 424242 under the evaluator's Bonferroni construction. Describe this construction rather than calling it a conventional single-comparator or seed-independent 95% interval.\",\n    \"Boundary comparison: constant-history MAE is 0.06436711309859705 and independently shifted-history MAE is 0.06856952089296987. Compare these conditions to the matched model-holdout condition, never to a pooled A/B primary mean; show the same fixed baselines alongside them. The shifted condition does not retain an advantage over the column-mean reference in the available arrays. This limitation belongs in the main results and conclusion.\",\n    \"Use sealed, common targets for the primary claim, with uniform targets within row, uniform rows within episode, equal checkpoint weights, equal statistical-seed weights and equal primary-condition weights. All-unqueried losses are a supplementary descriptive view because their evaluated target set changes with the method's selected queries.\",\n    \"No matched final residual-off, unconditional residual, donor-score-only, support-gating, missingness-stratified or cross-leaderboard ablation is available. Training diagnostic code is not outer empirical evidence. Do not fill this gap with search comparisons or invented results.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early overview: distinguish historical fitting/calibration from a held-out model's permitted queried scores and sealed targets; show where low-rank prediction and local residual transfer combine.\",\n    \"Figure 2, main accuracy comparison: two aligned panels for model and heuristic-family holdouts, four budget categories and four fixed method series; retain all five comparators in the adjacent main table.\",\n    \"Figure 3, evidence boundary: one grouped comparison of intact, constant and column-shifted history on the matched model-holdout target protocol, with proposed, column-mean and variance-selected low-rank predictors.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"method_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the sparse score matrix, budgeted queries, historical donor calibration and sealed-target boundary immediately legible without suggesting target access or measured component attribution.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"information_boundary\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; qualitative mechanism, not numerical evidence\",\n      \"split_by\": \"history-only fit and held-out-model inference\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"paper.sty sets a single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height; full_width is therefore the same physical span as the column, not a two-column figure*. Use two vertically stacked panels, each with at most five labeled objects, so 9-point text remains readable. Place on page 1 or 2, targeting 2.0-2.3 inches of artwork plus a concise caption, about one third of a page. Separate history calibration from inference with a visible boundary; do not shrink to a half-column thumbnail.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"budget_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Directly compare sealed-target error versus query budget for the studied method and fixed low-rank/linear references under both held-out conditions.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"line_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"method_family\",\n      \"metric\": \"fixed sealed-target mean absolute error in normalized score units; lower is better\",\n      \"split_by\": \"model/alias/duplicate holdout and heuristic-family/duplicate holdout\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use the full 5.5-inch single-column width for two side-by-side panels, at most two panels per row, one shared external legend, four budget ticks and four series: studied method, random-query low-rank, variance-query low-rank, linear ridge. Target 2.4-2.7 inches of artwork plus caption, roughly 0.4 page on pages 5-6. Use at least 8.5-point rendered labels and identical y limits. Seed standard deviations may be shown as descriptive error bars, not independent-sample confidence intervals; define any uncertainty drawn. If labels collide, stack the panels at the same width rather than shrinking text.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nwith open('references_provenance.json') as handle: refs=json.load(handle)['references']\nfor ref in refs: print(ref['citation_key']+' | '+ref['title']+' | '+ref.get('consulted_content',''))\nwith open('claims_grounding.json') as handle: claims=json.load(handle)['claims']\nprint('\\\\nLITERATURE CLAIMS')\nfor claim in claims:\n if claim.get('citation_keys'): print(json.dumps(claim,ensure_ascii=False))\nwith open('paper_strategy.json') as handle: strategy=json.load(handle)\nprint('\\\\nSTRATEGY LITERATURE NOTES')\nfor value in strategy['notes_for_writer']:\n if any(word in value.lower() for word in ['referenc','literature','novel','citat']):print(value)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nwith open('references_provenance.json') as handle: refs=json.load(handle)['references']\nfor ref in refs: print(ref['citation_key']+' | '+ref['title']+' | '+ref.get('consulted_content',''))\nwith open('claims_grounding.json') as handle: claims=json.load(handle)['claims']\nprint('\\\\nLITERATURE CLAIMS')\nfor claim in claims:\n if claim.get('citation_keys'): print(json.dumps(claim,ensure_ascii=False))\nwith open('paper_strategy.json') as handle: strategy=json.load(handle)\nprint('\\\\nSTRATEGY LITERATURE NOTES')\nfor value in strategy['notes_for_writer']:\n if any(word in value.lower() for word in ['referenc','literature','novel','citat']):print(value)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"MTEB | MTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark | abstract\nTinyBenchmarks | tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples | abstract\nMetaBench | metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models | abstract\nObservationalScaling | Observational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Langauge Model Performance | abstract\nSloth | Sloth: scaling laws for LLM skills to predict multi-benchmark performance across families | abstract\nKoren2009 | Matrix Factorization Techniques for Recommender Systems | abstract\nHu2008 | Collaborative Filtering for Implicit Feedback Datasets | abstract\nCandes2009 | Exact Matrix Completion via Convex Optimization | abstract\nJain2013 | Low-rank Matrix Completion using Alternating Minimization | abstract\nRennie2005 | Fast maximum margin matrix factorization for collaborative prediction | abstract\nKoenker2001 | Quantile Regression | abstract\nStone1977 | Consistent Nonparametric Regression | abstract\nBengio2004 | No Unbiased Estimator of the Variance of K-Fold Cross-Validation | abstract\nHoerl1970 | Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems | abstract\nChaloner1995 | Bayesian Experimental Design: A Review | abstract\nCohn1996 | Active Learning with Statistical Models | abstract\nRubin1976 | Inference and Missing Data | abstract\nEfron1979 | Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife | abstract\nCameron2008 | Bootstrap-Based Improvements for Inference with Clustered Errors | abstract\nTakacs2008 | Matrix factorization and neighbor based algorithms for the netflix prize problem | abstract\nMnih2007 | Probabilistic Matrix Factorization | abstract\nSalakhutdinov2008 | Bayesian probabilistic matrix factorization using Markov chain Monte Carlo | abstract\nCover1967 | Nearest neighbor pattern classification | abstract\nHarris2020 | Array programming with NumPy | abstract\nHunter2007 | Matplotlib: A 2D Graphics Environment | abstract\n\nLITERATURE CLAIMS\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: MTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4386576685\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MTEB\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/MTEB.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4392181972\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"TinyBenchmarks\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/TinyBenchmarks.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4406058560\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MetaBench\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/MetaBench.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Observational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Langauge Model Performance\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4415798639\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"ObservationalScaling\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/ObservationalScaling.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Sloth: scaling laws for LLM skills to predict multi-benchmark performance across families\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4405254278\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Sloth\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Sloth.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Matrix Factorization Techniques for Recommender Systems\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2054141820\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Koren2009\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Koren2009.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Collaborative Filtering for Implicit Feedback Datasets\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2101409192\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hu2008\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Hu2008.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Exact Matrix Completion via Convex Optimization\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2611328865\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Candes2009\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Candes2009.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Low-rank Matrix Completion using Alternating Minimization\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2952489973\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Jain2013\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Jain2013.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Fast maximum margin matrix factorization for collaborative prediction\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W1976618413\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Rennie2005\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Rennie2005.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Quantile Regression\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4241996101\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Koenker2001\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Koenker2001.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Consistent Nonparametric Regression\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2059507684\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Stone1977\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Stone1977.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: No Unbiased Estimator of the Variance of K-Fold Cross-Validation\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2998678080\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Bengio2004\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Bengio2004.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4234698323\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hoerl1970\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Hoerl1970.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Bayesian Experimental Design: A Review\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2014018052\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Chaloner1995\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Chaloner1995.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Active Learning with Statistical Models\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2949071206\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Cohn1996\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Cohn1996.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Inference and Missing Data\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2100358124\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Rubin1976\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Rubin1976.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2117897510\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Efron1979\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Efron1979.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Bootstrap-Based Improvements for Inference with Clustered Errors\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W3122178585\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Cameron2008\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Cameron2008.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Matrix factorization and neighbor based algorithms for the netflix prize problem\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2061460268\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Takacs2008\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Takacs2008.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Probabilistic Matrix Factorization\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2137245235\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Mnih2007\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Mnih2007.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Bayesian probabilistic matrix factorization using Markov chain Monte Carlo\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2085040216\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Salakhutdinov2008\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Salakhutdinov2008.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Nearest neighbor pattern classification\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2122111042\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Cover1967\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Cover1967.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Array programming with NumPy\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W3035965352\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Harris2020\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Harris2020.json\"]}\n{\"claim\": \"Literature positioning limited to consulted content: Matplotlib: A 2D Graphics Environment\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2011301426\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hunter2007\"], \"source_locations\": [\"literature_evidence/Hunter2007.json\"]}\n\nSTRATEGY LITERATURE NOTES\nICLR 2026 per VENUE.md: plan 8.5 substantive body pages, excluding references and appendix. Allocate 1.25 pages to title/abstract/introduction, 0.75 related work, 0.75 problem and information boundary, 1.75 method, 1.25 protocol, 1.75 results including controls, and 1.0 discussion/limitations/conclusion. Figure/table space is included in these allocations, not added on top. An eight-page body must end at least 75% full; never stretch spacing or invent experiments to fill it.\nUse only existing references.bib keys with synchronized provenance and actually consulted content. Current metadata supports candidate positioning around sparse matrix factorization and neighbor methods, not uninspected claims about literature findings. Do not pad related work with irrelevant recommender applications, and do not search for or infer the prohibited paper or its citation network. Approved starter works can be used only after permissible consultation and grounded citation retrieval.\nPlan the appendix after references with the substantive sections listed above plus the separate LLM Usage disclosure. Keep core control limitations, primary outcomes and uncertainty in the body. Do not invent future posthoc rankings; route later authoritative appendix-ranked material only when it actually arrives.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
