{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor key in ['TinyBenchmarks','MetaBench','ObservationalScaling','Sloth','Takacs2008','Cohn1996']:\n data=json.loads(Path('literature_evidence',key+'.json').read_text())\n print(key, 'keys:',list(data))\n abstract=data.get('abstract')\n if not abstract:\n  inverted=data.get('abstract_inverted_index',{})\n  positions={index:word for word,indices in inverted.items() for index in indices}\n  abstract=' '.join(positions[index] for index in sorted(positions))\n if abstract: print(abstract)\nPY\nsed -n '262,348p' paper.tex; tail -n 20 memory.md; ls global_memory/conclusions*; sed -n '1,80p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor key in ['TinyBenchmarks','MetaBench','ObservationalScaling','Sloth','Takacs2008','Cohn1996']:\n data=json.loads(Path('literature_evidence',key+'.json').read_text())\n print(key, 'keys:',list(data))\n abstract=data.get('abstract')\n if not abstract:\n  inverted=data.get('abstract_inverted_index',{})\n  positions={index:word for word,indices in inverted.items() for index in indices}\n  abstract=' '.join(positions[index] for index in sorted(positions))\n if abstract: print(abstract)\nPY\nsed -n '262,348p' paper.tex; tail -n 20 memory.md; ls global_memory/conclusions*; sed -n '1,80p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\"","aggregated_output":"TinyBenchmarks keys: ['id', 'doi', 'title', 'publication_year', 'authorships', 'abstract_inverted_index', 'primary_location', 'biblio', 'type', 'display_name', 'publication_date', 'referenced_works', 'retrieval_url']\nThe versatility of large language models (LLMs) led to the creation of diverse benchmarks that thoroughly test a variety of language models' abilities. These benchmarks consist of tens of thousands of examples making evaluation of LLMs very expensive. In this paper, we investigate strategies to reduce the number of evaluations needed to assess the performance of an LLM on several key benchmarks. For example, we show that to accurately estimate the performance of an LLM on MMLU, a popular multiple-choice QA benchmark consisting of 14K examples, it is sufficient to evaluate this LLM on 100 curated examples. We release evaluation tools and tiny versions of popular benchmarks: Open LLM Leaderboard, MMLU, HELM, and AlpacaEval 2.0. Our empirical analysis demonstrates that these tools and tiny benchmarks are sufficient to reliably and efficiently reproduce the original evaluation results.\nMetaBench keys: ['id', 'doi', 'title', 'publication_year', 'authorships', 'abstract_inverted_index', 'primary_location', 'biblio', 'type', 'display_name', 'publication_date', 'referenced_works', 'retrieval_url']\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) vary in their abilities on a range of tasks. Initiatives such as the Open LLM Leaderboard aim to quantify these differences with several large benchmarks (sets of test items to which an LLM can respond either correctly or incorrectly). However, high correlations within and between benchmark scores suggest that (1) there exists a small set of common underlying abilities that these benchmarks measure, and (2) items tap into redundant information and the benchmarks may thus be considerably compressed. We use data from n > 5000 LLMs to identify the most informative items of six benchmarks, ARC, GSM8K, HellaSwag, MMLU, TruthfulQA and WinoGrande (with d = 28,632 items in total). From them we distill a sparse benchmark, metabench, that has less than 3% of the original size of all six benchmarks combined. This new sparse benchmark goes beyond point scores by yielding estimators of the underlying benchmark-specific abilities. We show that these estimators (1) can be used to reconstruct each original individual benchmark score with, on average, 1.24% root mean square error (RMSE), (2) reconstruct the original total score with 0.58% RMSE, and (3) have a single underlying common factor whose Spearman correlation with the total score is r = 0.94.\nObservationalScaling keys: ['id', 'doi', 'title', 'publication_year', 'authorships', 'abstract_inverted_index', 'primary_location', 'biblio', 'type', 'display_name', 'publication_date', 'referenced_works', 'retrieval_url']\nUnderstanding how language model performance varies with scale is critical to benchmark and algorithm development. Scaling laws are one approach to building this understanding, but the requirement of training models across many different scales has limited their use. We propose an alternative, observational approach that bypasses model training and instead builds scaling laws from ~100 publically available models. Building a single scaling law from multiple model families is challenging due to large variations in their training compute efficiencies and capabilities. However, we show that these variations are consistent with a simple, generalized scaling law where language model performance is a function of a low-dimensional capability space, and model families only vary in their efficiency in converting training compute to capabilities. Using this approach, we show the surprising predictability of complex scaling phenomena: we show that several emergent phenomena follow a smooth, sigmoidal behavior and are predictable from small models; we show that the agent performance of models such as GPT-4 can be precisely predicted from simpler non-agentic benchmarks; and we show how to predict the impact of post-training interventions like Chain-of-Thought and Self-Consistency as language model capabilities continue to improve.\nSloth keys: ['id', 'doi', 'title', 'publication_year', 'authorships', 'abstract_inverted_index', 'primary_location', 'biblio', 'type', 'display_name', 'publication_date', 'referenced_works', 'retrieval_url']\nScaling laws for large language models (LLMs) predict model performance based on parameters like size and training data. However, differences in training configurations and data processing across model families lead to significant variations in benchmark performance, making it difficult for a single scaling law to generalize across all LLMs. On the other hand, training family-specific scaling laws requires training models of varying sizes for every family. In this work, we propose Skills Scaling Laws (SSLaws, pronounced as Sloth), a novel scaling law that leverages publicly available benchmark data and assumes LLM performance is driven by low-dimensional latent skills, such as reasoning and instruction following. These latent skills are influenced by computational resources like model size and training tokens, but with varying efficiencies across model families. Sloth exploits correlations across benchmarks to provide more accurate and interpretable predictions while alleviating the need to train multiple LLMs per family. We present both theoretical results on parameter identification and empirical evaluations on 12 prominent benchmarks, from Open LLM Leaderboard v1/v2, demonstrating that Sloth predicts LLM performance accurately and offers insights into scaling behaviors for complex downstream tasks, increased test-time compute, and compute-optimal scaling of skills.\nTakacs2008 keys: ['id', 'doi', 'title', 'publication_year', 'authorships', 'abstract_inverted_index', 'primary_location', 'biblio', 'type', 'display_name', 'publication_date', 'referenced_works', 'retrieval_url']\nCollaborative filtering (CF) approaches proved to be effective for recommender systems in predicting user preferences in item selection using known user ratings of items. This subfield of machine learning has gained a lot of popularity with the Netflix Prize competition started in October 2006. Two major approaches for this problem are matrix factorization (MF) and the neighbor based approach (NB). In this work, we propose various variants of MF and NB that can boost the performance of the usual ensemble based scheme. First, we investigate various regularization scenarios for MF. Second, we introduce two NB methods: one is based on correlation coefficients and the other on linear least squares. At the experimentation part, we show that the proposed approaches compare favorably with existing ones in terms of prediction accuracy and/or required training time. We present results of blending the proposed methods.\nCohn1996 keys: ['id', 'doi', 'title', 'publication_year', 'authorships', 'abstract_inverted_index', 'primary_location', 'biblio', 'type', 'display_name', 'publication_date', 'referenced_works', 'retrieval_url']\nFor many types of machine learning algorithms, one can compute the statistically `optimal' way to select training data. In this paper, we review how optimal data selection techniques have been used with feedforward neural networks. We then show how the same principles may be used to select data for two alternative, statistically-based learning architectures: mixtures of Gaussians and locally weighted regression. While the techniques for neural networks are computationally expensive and approximate, the techniques for mixtures of Gaussians and locally weighted regression are both efficient and accurate. Empirically, we observe that the optimality criterion sharply decreases the number of training examples the learner needs in order to achieve good performance.\nBlend & $0,\\ 0.25,\\ 0.5,\\ 1$ \\\\\nDistance scale floor & $0.025$ \\\\\nNegligible total weight & $10^{-12}$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Implementation settings, not empirically isolated optimal choices. Historical pool size is capped by the expression in the main text.}\n\\label{tab:settings}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L51-L297]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L920-L939]]\n\\end{table}\n\nFor each validation fold, remove its rows from the historical fitting pool. Fit the auxiliary base on the remaining rows, select a donor pool from those rows, and split donors into two folds. For each donor fold, fit without that fold, retaining only observed donor scores. For a validation row, simulate the same acquisition criterion on its available scores. Cache donor-excluded predictions for each queried index tuple; the cache affects computation but not access to targets. At each reporting budget, evaluate the clipped base plus each candidate correction on finite unqueried validation cells. Pool absolute-error sums and target counts across these cells and folds, select bandwidth/shrinkage/blend, and rebuild the donor bank from historical data. If support checks fail, retain the zero-blend configuration. Auxiliary randomness uses a separate seed sequence, preserving the main-fit random stream.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L110-L297]]\n\nThe code also calculates unconditional residual and direct donor-score diagnostics during this process. No outer results for those choices are retained. Neither their existence nor historical selection of a nonzero correction demonstrates an outer improvement relative to a fully matched zero-correction configuration. The main estimator's clipped base and donor-specific auxiliary bases are not identical fitted models, so fold exclusion should not be interpreted as an exact decomposition of outer prediction error.\n\n\\subsection{Complete primary comparisons}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}\n\\toprule\nHoldout & Estimator & $k=1$ & $k=2$ & $k=4$ & $k=8$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nModel & Column mean & 0.06437 & 0.06437 & 0.06437 & 0.06437 \\\\\nModel & Query mean & 0.25893 & 0.24443 & 0.23056 & 0.22051 \\\\\nModel & Random low-rank & 0.05498 & 0.04965 & 0.04403 & 0.03877 \\\\\nModel & Variance low-rank & 0.04986 & 0.04713 & 0.04305 & 0.03914 \\\\\nModel & Linear ridge & 0.05010 & 0.04694 & 0.04416 & 0.03927 \\\\\nModel & Residual transfer & 0.04258 & 0.03943 & 0.03677 & 0.03448 \\\\\n\\midrule\nFamily & Column mean & 0.06411 & 0.06411 & 0.06411 & 0.06411 \\\\\nFamily & Query mean & 0.25572 & 0.23810 & 0.23156 & 0.21710 \\\\\nFamily & Random low-rank & 0.05664 & 0.05312 & 0.04611 & 0.04146 \\\\\nFamily & Variance low-rank & 0.05192 & 0.05042 & 0.04594 & 0.04152 \\\\\nFamily & Linear ridge & 0.05012 & 0.04731 & 0.04426 & 0.03984 \\\\\nFamily & Residual transfer & 0.04789 & 0.04499 & 0.04251 & 0.03782 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Complete seed-averaged sealed-target MAE by holdout and budget. Low-rank is abbreviated LR in the seed table. All values derive from the same retained row/checkpoint arrays.}\n\\end{table}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Col. mean & Query mean & Random LR & Var. LR & Ridge & Studied \\\\\n\\midrule\n6841 & 0.05946 & 0.23783 & 0.04536 & 0.04282 & 0.04166 & 0.03850 \\\\\n8089 & 0.06262 & 0.23947 & 0.04751 & 0.04596 & 0.04594 & 0.04113 \\\\\n9011 & 0.07063 & 0.23404 & 0.05142 & 0.04959 & 0.04815 & 0.04280 \\\\\n\\midrule\nSeed SD & 0.00575 & 0.00278 & 0.00307 & 0.00339 & 0.00330 & 0.00217 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Sealed-target MAE averaged equally over primary holdouts and budgets within each seed; SD is descriptive sample standard deviation. Shared source data preclude interpreting these as independent dataset replications.}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe baseline-specific component-bootstrap bounds, in the order column mean, query mean, random low-rank, variance low-rank, and linear ridge, are respectively $[0.01538,0.04037]$, $[0.18530,0.20768]$, $[0.00412,0.01426]$, $[0.00340,0.00920]$, and $[0.00210,0.00939]$. They bound baseline-minus-studied errors under the retained construction. Taking lower and upper minima separately produces the reported pair; it does not select a single baseline interval. The evaluator's zero-weight-split convention inserts a conservative negative gain for such a bootstrap split. The reproduction script preserves that convention rather than silently dropping the split.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/baseline_gain_bounds]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L999-L1017]]\n\n\\subsection{Changing-target diagnostic and disruption interpretation}\nThe evaluator also records loss over all observed but currently unqueried cells. Seed- and budget-averaged values for the studied package are 0.03665 on model holdout, 0.04242 on family holdout, 0.06528 with constant history, and 0.06352 with shifted history. This diagnostic changes its evaluated cell set as acquisitions accumulate and can also differ across query policies. It is therefore not used for the principal comparative claim. In particular, its shifted-history value cannot be substituted for the sealed-target control result.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/changing_target_mae]]\n\nIn the constant control, all methods using only centered historical linear variation collapse to the same column means on sealed targets in the retained arrays. The query-mean reference remains different because it uses real new-model query values rather than the collapsed historical values. Column shifting preserves each column's observed scores and count, including the value/mask pairing, but not the row-wise alignment. These controls distinguish information in per-column distributions from information in cross-column relationships at the package level; they do not isolate a correction mechanism or prove that every relationship has been destroyed.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/cases/C/mean]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L808-L830]]\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility and incomplete evidence}\nThe accompanying \\texttt{README.md} maps source files, commands, dependencies, and data versions. Readable snapshots preserve the evaluated estimator, the original predictor, and the final evaluator. Figures and numeric tables are generated by \\texttt{build\\_paper\\_artifacts.py} from the immutable evidence registry, using NumPy for array computations and Matplotlib for quantitative plots \\citep{Harris2020,Hunter2007}. The conceptual overview is a qualitative diagram with separate rendering provenance. No schematic numeric values are used as experimental results. Every quantitative display has an input trail; source locations support implementation claims separately from file fingerprints.\n\nDevelopment selection was adaptive and preceded freezing the final configuration. Two development configurations had numeric scores; a support-gating configuration had no retained numeric score after evaluation failure. The failure diagnostics do not identify whether that failure was statistical, implementation-related, or due to another condition. It is missing performance evidence, not a measured loss, and neither development comparison is a matched final ablation. This paper uses the independent final evaluation for its quantitative comparisons. No additional ablation, temporal holdout, missingness stratification, or cross-leaderboard study was completed for the manuscript.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/result.total_evaluated_programs]]\n\nRetrieved reference metadata and consulted abstracts are retained locally with provenance links. The literature discussion is limited to that consulted content and does not claim an exhaustive search or establish priority.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLanguage models assisted with research hypothesis formulation, implementation drafting, reference-query planning, manuscript framing, writing, and evidence/validation code. The paper-writing assistant inspected the retained implementation and evaluator, generated deterministic summaries and plots from retained arrays, and revised the manuscript against compilation and grounding checks. The numerical model-evaluation evidence comes from the frozen evaluator, not language-model-generated measurements. Language-model assistance does not provide independent verification of novelty or statistical validity. The conceptual-figure workflow may use model-assisted rendering; this does not introduce additional empirical evidence.\n\n\\end{document}\n- summary: paper_strategy_ready: wrote and checked both strategy outputs; selected scoped prediction-and-limits framing over campaign chronology or unsupported cross-domain/residual-causality stories; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No new scientific experiments or causal evidence were produced.\n- evidence: Independent final.qualification.status=completed and outcome=qualified; downstream_study and prior-paper/edit inputs absent. Main claim retains final MAE 0.040807843083862115 versus baseline minimum 0.045249969215420516, budget comparisons, paired component-bound construction and matched disruption controls. Inspected selected residual-transfer source and evaluator directly; no matched outer residual-off ablation. Planned three readable figures at inspected 5.5-inch single-column geometry, 8.5 substantive body pages, six grounded supplement sections and LLM disclosure. JSON contract, source IDs, readability budgets, stage boundaries and final aggregate reconstruction passed using the Python standard library. Initial memory helper rejected the global_memory directory symlink; resolved canonical paths repaired global logging. NumPy was absent in the active Python; no dependency installation or experiment was needed. No apply_patch executable/tool was exposed, so an in-command apply_patch Add File shim applied the strategy patches. No network access, final manuscript, figure generation or final-paper validation occurred.\n- next_action: Hand the checked strategy to the standalone writer; preserve observed-support limitations, controls, qualification disclosure and lack of component ablations; verify permissible citation content and official template provenance before final drafting.\n- tags: stop_reason=paper_strategy_ready, thesis_first, final_evaluation_independent, qualification_preserved, no_new_experiments, no_final_paper_validation\n\n### 2026-11-17T13:45:48.876891+00:00 | step=paper | scope=paper\n- summary: Confirmed scoped prediction-and-limits thesis and independent qualified final outcome; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No new experiment or mechanism-isolating evidence.\n- evidence: paper_data.json final.qualification.status/outcome and final.evidence.episodes; selected source and evaluator inspected. Existing 25-reference retrieval is dominated by tangential recommender applications and includes duplicated works; replace with exact-identity classics and approved starter titles, consulting abstracts without citation chains. apply_patch executable absent; use a local compatible patch helper for manuscript additions.\n- next_action: Stage readable evaluated sources, generate derived tables and quantitative figures, draft and validate anonymized ICLR manuscript.\n\n### 2026-11-17T14:00:59.529460+00:00 | step=draft | scope=paper\n- summary: Drafted scoped manuscript and reconstructed all final aggregates and bootstrap bounds without new experiments; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Manuscript analysis adds no mechanism-isolating scientific result.\n- evidence: paper.tex; derived_results.json; build_paper_artifacts.py; 55 mapped claims; 25 consulted reference abstracts. Source-defined ALS/query/residual details are staged readably. Rejected causal residual attribution, sufficient-budget, genuinely-missing-cell and generative-model claims. Three figure groups follow strategy; concept PNG is an orchestrator-owned guarded placeholder. Zero dangling evidence refs. Official ICLR 2026 template fetched directly. Wrong inverse-matrix DOI and tangential prior retrieval were replaced, not cited. Source-only SI gate required embedding generated supplementary tables. Local natbib/fancyhdr support copies were archived to use system packages and avoid unsupported dynamic-import checks.\n- next_action: Complete rendered layout, schema and claim validation; record canonical-validator environment limitations precisely.\n\n### 2026-11-17T14:08:26.400957+00:00 | step=final | scope=paper\n- summary: paper_draft_ready: paper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with grounded inputs, preserving qualified final status, observed-cell scope, unfavorable column-shift control and absent mechanism ablations; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No new scientific experiment or causal evidence was introduced.\n- evidence: Final PDF has 8 body pages, 2 reference pages and 3 supplementary pages; rendered last-body-page geometric occupancy 0.9963. 55 claims and 25 consulted references pass canonical claim/reference checks. Scientific-evidence final report: 200 refs, 121 resolved, 79 anchored, zero dangling; 14 provenance sidecars. Source snapshots byte-match evaluated originals. Quantitative figure schema/density checks pass. Restored official bundled fancyhdr for correct running headers; system natbib avoids unsupported local dynamic-import scan. Canonical figure validator lacks src.orchestrator; canonical compile-input gate rejects Debian texmf.cnf symlink, while direct compilation, resolved-input check and rendered SI validation pass. Concept overview remains the explicitly authorized guarded placeholder pending synchronous orchestrator rendering. No new evaluation or prohibited-literature access occurred.\n- next_action: Orchestrator should render the required overview, recompile and run its authoritative figure/page/input gates; retain all limitations and qualification facts during any revision.\n\nglobal_memory/conclusions.md\nglobal_memory/conclusions_history.jsonl\nglobal_memory/conclusions_other_workers.md\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n\nTwo modes:\n\n* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n  global) plus one JSONL record to ``memory.jsonl``. Existing behavior.\n* ``--write-conclusions`` — atomically replaces ``conclusions.md`` with a\n  validated single-paragraph distilled summary and appends a record to\n  ``conclusions_history.jsonl``. Validation rejects empty input,\n  multi-paragraph input, or input over 300 words.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport stat\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nCONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT = 300\nGATE_TRIGGER_VALUES = (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\")\n\nLOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS = \"\"\"# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n\"\"\"\n\nGLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER = \"# Global Memory Log\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef utc_now() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the current UTC timestamp string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n\n\ndef _load_event(*, event_json: str | None, event_file: Path | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load event.\"\"\"\n    if event_json:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_json)\n    elif event_file is not None:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    else:\n        raw = input().strip()\n        if not raw:\n            raise ValueError(\"missing event payload\")\n        parsed = json.loads(raw)\n    if not isinstance(parsed, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"event payload must be a JSON object\")\n    return parsed\n\n\ndef _ensure_required_fields(event: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Ensure required fields.\"\"\"\n    normalized = dict(event)\n    if not isinstance(normalized.get(\"timestamp\"), str) or not normalized[\"timestamp\"].strip():\n        normalized[\"timestamp\"] = utc_now()\n\n    required_string_fields = (\"step\", \"scope\", \"summary\", \"evidence\", \"next_action\")\n    for key in required_string_fields:\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing required string field: {key}\")\n        normalized[key] = value.strip()\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if value is None:\n            continue\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized[key] = str(value)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
